From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 08:51:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: msm8998: give the CPU OPPs the vendor's
 measured energy model

msm8998 is an asymmetric big.LITTLE part, but the scheduler has never had an
energy model for it, so EAS never starts: /sys/kernel/debug/energy_model is
created and stays empty. qcom-cpufreq-hw does set .register_em to
cpufreq_register_em_with_opp(), but that resolves to
dev_pm_opp_of_register_em(), which needs either opp-microwatt or
"dynamic-power-coefficient" plus known voltages. These OPP tables carry
opp-level -- a CPRh corner -- and neither, so registration fails silently and
the scheduler treats a 2.36 GHz A73 and a 1.9 GHz A53 as interchangeable
capacity.

Downstream shipped a measured model for this exact SoC. msm-4.4's
arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/msm8998.dtsi carries an energy-costs node whose
busy-cost-data gives <capacity power> pairs per cluster, with capacity
normalised so 1024 is the gold cluster at its top OPP:

  CPU_COST_0 (silver, 22 points):   65 -> 11   ...   419 -> 201
  CPU_COST_1 (gold,   31 points):  129 -> 56   ...  1024 -> 1683
  CLUSTER_COST_0: 65 -> 17 ... 419 -> 66
  CLUSTER_COST_1: 129 -> 24 ... 1024 -> 203

Within a cluster capacity is proportional to frequency, so each curve maps
onto these tables directly; the silver curve has exactly as many points as
this table has OPPs, and the gold curve has one more (its 1021 point sits
between our top two). Values here are the curves interpolated at each OPP's
frequency.

Each OPP is charged CPU_COST plus CLUSTER_COST/4, so a fully occupied cluster
sums to downstream's own cluster total. The unit scale is 0.384 mW, taken from
a measured 8-thread burn on a Pixel 2 XL: 4x gold@1024 + 4x silver@419 plus
both cluster terms is 7805 units and drew ~3.0 W over idle. That puts a gold
core at 666 mW at 2.36 GHz and a silver core at 84 mW at 1.9 GHz, and the two
tables now cost the same at equal capacity -- 209 versus 217 units at 419 --
which is the design point the downstream curves encode.

The shape is what matters to the scheduler, and the shape is the useful part:
gold costs 8.6x the power for 2.44x the capacity between 419 and 1024.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
